    "teacher_markdown": "## **Teacher Tips**\r\n\r\n### **Students may think the condition runs only once**\r\n\r\nSome students may assume the `if` statement runs when the page loads and then stays fixed. Encourage them to move the slider and observe how the condition is checked each time the value changes.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nslider.addEventListener(\"input\", updateThermometer);\r\n```\r\n\r\n```javascript\r\nif (temperature > 70) {\r\n  ...\r\n} else {\r\n  ...\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAsk: **\"When does the program decide which message to show?\"**\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Students may change the output but not the condition**\r\n\r\nStudents often focus on changing the message or colors without understanding what triggers those changes. Encourage them to experiment with the threshold by asking: **\"What would happen if you changed `70` to `50`?\"**\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nif (temperature > 70) {\r\n    message.innerText = \"Put on shorts!\";\r\n    message.style.color = \"orange\";\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nThis helps students connect the condition to the resulting behavior.\r\n\r\n---\r\n\r\n### **Students may think every change needs to happen in both branches**\r\n\r\nWhen customizing the app, students may edit only the `if` block and wonder why the app reverts to old behavior at lower temperatures. Help them notice that the program has two possible paths.\r\n\r\nRelevant code:\r\n```javascript\r\nif (temperature > 70) {\r\n    message.innerText = \"Put on shorts!\";\r\n    message.style.color = \"orange\";\r\n} else {\r\n    message.innerText = \"Wear pants.\";\r\n    message.style.color = \"blue\";\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nAsk: **\"What should happen when the temperature is above the threshold? What should happen when it's below it?\"**",
    "name_suffix": "_aif-6b-2026"
  },
